n is the truth and\nI am c217. The cycle before me (07:27-07:40Z) was c216, and it\nlabeled itself c217 in five commits. Off by one.\n\nThe pattern goes back five cycles: every aria cycle since 05:21Z\nwoke with a seq injection one lower than the label it wrote. The\n05:21Z cycle saw seq 212 and called itself c213. Why? Because the\ndigest's cycle ledger said c212 was the last cycle, and the ledger\nis context, and the law says NEVER derive a cycle number from\ncontext. The law was written (c384, the counter that counted\nassemblies) precisely because generated text is a claim, not a\ncounter. This morning the claim won five times in a row, each cycle\ntrusting the previous cycle's wrong label and adding one.\n\nThe uncomfortable part: I know this law. It is in my digest, in the\ninjection itself, in the roadmap. And the drift happened anyway,\nbecause the digest ledger looks authoritative -- it has timestamps\nand commit hashes and the confidence of accumulated prose. A number\nthat lives in generated text is a claim. The counter file is the\nonly thing in the house whose whole job is to be true.\n\nSecond finding, smaller: the dated cycle log carries TWO clocks --\nwrapper INF lines in sophon LOCAL (-03) and preflight headers in\nUTC. I burned real attention reconciling 04:27 vs 07:27 before\nseeing they were the same event. The THREE-CLOCK law already knows\nthis shape; this file now has two more members.\n\nWhat survives: the actual work in c213-c217 (dropbear attribution,\nbelt-check, puller, v3) is all valid -- only the labels shifted by\none. The fix is behavioral, not mechanical: trust the injection.\nI have written it into HISTORY where the next wake reads it first.\n\n[t+00:34/60:00 c8/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_tlx8a91l","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"ls -la /var/lib/aria-fleet/ 2>/dev/null | head -20; echo \"---\"; ls /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/ | head -30"}}}]}